{"created": "2026-08-25T03:21:16.581022+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-10T21:39:06.436051+00:00", "id": 22089373, "conceptrecid": "22089372", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.22089372", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "metadata": {"title": "Strong Tree-Childness Is a Sharp Generic-Identifiability Boundary for Level-2 Jukes-Cantor Networks", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "publication_date": "2026-08-23", "description": "<p>Reticulate evolutionary histories encode hybridization, recombination, and lateral transfer, but such histories are scientifically recoverable from sequence data only when distinct networks induce distinguishable site-pattern distributions. We determine this information limit for binary, LSA-rootable, already-simple semi-directed strongly tree-child level-2 networks under the paper's one-step reticulation-preserving convention and the open four-state Jukes&ndash;Cantor model. A source-relative full-dimensional regular stochastic containment occurs if and only if the labelled reduced bridge trees agree and every pair of corresponding blobs is labelled-isomorphic or differs by ordinary triangle redirection. Hence no proper one-sided generic containment exists, and an exact generic distribution determines a unique topology modulo that redirection. Fourier cut ranks recover the bridge tree, while positive rank-one factorizations recover only projective local tensors modulo the full incidence-scaling group; physical bridge multipliers are not identified. A graph-to-algebra classification of cycle and theta factors, together with marginal submersions and coherent bounded probes, reconstructs arbitrary subdivisions.</p>\n<p>The boundary is sharp even without triangles. For every n &ge; 4 we construct two triangle-free networks that are weakly but not strongly tree-child, nonisomorphic, and not triangle-related, yet whose open JC images share a regular relatively open subset of full dimension 2n + 1. A second triangle-containing family exhibits a distinct pendant-transfer mechanism. All finite-atlas, class-membership, Fourier-equality, sign, and Jacobian claims are supported by exact primary and separately implemented replay certificates.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Kriebel, Alec", "affiliation": "Independent Researcher", "orcid": "0009-0001-9320-500X"}], "keywords": ["phylogenetic networks", "reticulate evolution", "identifiability", "Jukes-Cantor model", "level-2 networks", "strongly tree-child networks", "algebraic statistics", "generic identifiability", "directed containment", "semi-directed networks", "group-based models", "phylogenetic invariants", "Fourier coordinates", "triangle redirection", "bridge tree reconstruction"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22064121", "relation": "isSupplementedBy", "resource_type": "dataset", "scheme": "doi"}], "version": "1.1.7", "language": "eng", "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "22089372"}}]}}, "title": "Strong Tree-Childness Is a Sharp Generic-Identifiability Boundary for Level-2 Jukes-Cantor Networks", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/22089373", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/22089373", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/22089373?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.22089373", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/22089373/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/22089372", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/22089372", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22089372", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.22089372", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/a